      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1233,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen, the oxygen atom pulls the shared electrons closer to itself. This creates a slight negative charge on the oxygen side and a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side. This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself and other things (a property called **cohesion** and **adhesion**).\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, a liquid, and a gas under standard temperatures and pressures.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules move slowly and form a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** This is the state we use for drinking and washing. The molecules are close together but can slide past one another.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast that they escape into the air.\n\n### 3. Unique Physical Properties\nWater has several properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water has a \"skin\" on its surface that allows insects to walk on it and for capillary action to pull water up through plants.\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This allows it to transport nutrients and minerals through the environment and our bodies.\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps the temperature of the oceans stable, which is vital for marine life.\n\n### 4. Abundance\nWater is the most abundant compound on the surface of the Earth. It covers about 71% of the planet and makes up about 60% of the human body. It is found in oceans, lakes, rivers, clouds, and even inside rocks deep underground."
